<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n103440" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τετρά̆-μετρος</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>,
			<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>consisting of four
			metres</i>, i.e., in iambic and trochaic verse, <i>consisting of
				four double feet</i> or <i>syzygies</i>: <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τὸ τετράμετρον</span> is generally <i>the
				trochaic tetrameter</i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Nu.</u>
				642
			</span></i>,<i><span class="en">
				645
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				X.
				<u>Smp.</u>
				6.3
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Arist.
				<u>Rh.</u>
				1404a31
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				1409a1
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Po.</u>
				1459b37
			</span></i>: also <i>the anapaestic tetrameter</i>, called <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τὸ Ἀριστοφάνειον</span> (as in <i><span class="en">
				<u>Nu.</u>
				957
			</span></i> sq.), <i><span class="en">
				D.H.
				<u>Comp.</u>
				25
			</span></i>; cf. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τρίμετρος</span>.
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">γωνίαι τ</span>. <i>square</i>, i.e.
				<i>right</i>, angles, <i><span class="en">
				Callix.
				2
			</span></i> (dub. l.).</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
